## 6. Default and Keyword Arguments

Functions can define default parameter values and accept keyword arguments.

```python
def introduce(name, course="Python"):
    return f"{name} is learning {course}"


print(introduce("Anudeep"))
print(introduce("Anudeep", course="Core Python"))
```

Default values are used when an argument is omitted. Keyword arguments identify parameters by name.

## 7. Breaking Problems into Reusable Functions

A large problem is easier to understand, test, and maintain when it is divided into smaller functions.

### Example: Student Grade Calculator

```python
def calculate_average(marks):
    if not marks:
        raise ValueError("Marks cannot be empty")

    return sum(marks) / len(marks)


def calculate_grade(average):
    if average >= 90:
        return "A"
    elif average >= 70:
        return "B"
    elif average >= 50:
        return "C"
    else:
        return "Needs improvement"


def main():
    marks = [80, 75, 90]

    average = calculate_average(marks)
    grade = calculate_grade(average)

    print(f"Average: {average:.2f}")
    print(f"Grade: {grade}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

**Expected output:**

```text
Average: 81.67
Grade: B
```


